<CDName>private_reading1</CDName>
<CDBase>http://www.openmath.org/cd</CDBase>
<CDReviewDate>2027-10-03</CDReviewDate>
<CDDate>2026-10-03</CDDate>
<CDVersion>1</CDVersion>
<CDRevision>0</CDRevision>
<CDStatus>private</CDStatus>
<CDComment>
  Author: Scivics Lab
  SourceURL: https://github.com/scivicslab/openmath-lisp
</CDComment>

<Description>
  Symbols that record how one formula's LaTeX was read, for the cases a letter means one thing in one
  formula of a document and another thing elsewhere in the same document. Each symbol is an attribution
  key: it says nothing about the value of the attributed object, so an application may ignore it.
</Description>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>vectors</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is a list of variables that the attributed formula's LaTeX wrote as vectors.
  A dot between two of them was read as a scalar product and a cross as a vector product.
</Description>
<Example>
  The LaTeX (i \cdot I)I + (i \cdot J)J, read in a document where the letter i elsewhere means the
  imaginary unit, carries vectors with the list i, j, I, J.
</Example>
</CDDefinition>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>euler</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is the variable that the attributed formula's LaTeX wrote for the base of the
  natural logarithm. A power of that letter was read as transc1:exp.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>imaginary</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is the variable that the attributed formula's LaTeX wrote for the imaginary
  unit. That letter was read as nums1:i.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>time</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is the variable that a dot over a quantity differentiates by in the attributed
  formula's LaTeX.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>coordinates</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is a list of variables whose dotted form the attributed formula's LaTeX used as
  an independent variable rather than as a derivative.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>functions</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
  The attribution value is a list of lists, each giving a quantity of the attributed formula followed by
  the variables it depends on. Each occurrence of that quantity was read as an application to them.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>

</CD>
